## DEFECT-002 — Doctor records can be modified/deleted with no authentication

**Severity:** Critical
**Related test:** API-09
**Status:** Open

**Endpoint:** `DELETE /api/Doctors/{id}` (and by the same code pattern, `PUT /api/Doctors/{id}`)

**Steps to reproduce:**
1. Send `DELETE https://hospital-manager-deploy.onrender.com/api/Doctors/999999` with **no `Authorization` header at all**.
2. Observe the response.

**Expected result:** `401 Unauthorized` — the request should be rejected before the server even looks up the record, since no credentials were supplied.

**Actual result:** `404 Not Found` (RFC 9110 Problem Details response). This confirms the request was processed by the controller action itself — no authorization check intercepted it. If the endpoint were properly protected, the framework would return `401` before the doctor lookup runs, regardless of whether that ID exists.

**Root cause (source review, `DoctorsController.cs`):**
```csharp
[HttpGet]                          // public — by design, confirmed
[HttpGet("{id:int}")]              // public — by design, confirmed
[Authorize]
[HttpPost]                         // protected — correct
[HttpPut("{id:int}")]              // NOT protected — no [Authorize] above this action
[HttpDelete("{id:int}")]           // NOT protected — no [Authorize] above this action
```
Only the `POST` (create) action carries `[Authorize]`. `PUT` (update) and `DELETE` have no authorization attribute at either the class or method level.

**Impact:** Any unauthenticated person can modify or permanently delete any doctor's record in the system.

**Reproduced a second time** (independent request, different `traceId: 00-86d870b6287caf9fb6f156170394a218-3e563baaa9f716aa-00`), same result — `404 Not Found`, no `401` challenge. Confirms the behavior is consistent, not a one-off.

**Reproduced a third time with a real doctor ID** (not a nonexistent one) — request returned `500 Internal Server Error` instead of `404`/`401`. This is the strongest confirmation yet: the request reached the database layer and failed there, which is only possible if no authorization check intercepted it earlier. See DEFECT-004 below for the crash itself.

**Suggested fix direction:** Add `[Authorize]` (scoped to the appropriate role, e.g. Admin) to both the `PUT` and `DELETE` actions in `DoctorsController`.

**Assigned to:** Backend Engineer, Security Engineer

