## DEFECT-004 — Deleting a doctor with related records crashes with an unhandled 500

**Severity:** High
**Related test:** API-09 (follow-up)
**Status:** Open — root cause inferred from source; exact exception body not yet captured

**Endpoint:** `DELETE /api/Doctors/{id}`

**Steps to reproduce:**
1. `DELETE https://hospital-manager-deploy.onrender.com/api/Doctors/1` (an existing doctor with likely-linked appointment data from prior deployment verification testing — see `docs/DEPLOYMENT_BACKEND.md`).
2. Observe the response.

**Actual result:** `500 Internal Server Error` (bare status reported; exact response body not yet captured — recommended follow-up: re-run and paste the full body to confirm the exact exception).

**Root cause (source review, `DoctorsController.cs`):**
```csharp
[HttpDelete("{id:int}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Delete(int id)
{
    var doctor = await _context.Doctors.FindAsync(id);
    if (doctor is null) return NotFound();
    _context.Doctors.Remove(doctor);
    await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    return NoContent();
}
```
No check for related `Appointment` or `MedicalRecord` rows before deleting, and no `try`/`catch` around `SaveChangesAsync()`. If the doctor has related records, SQL Server rejects the delete on a foreign key constraint, EF Core throws a `DbUpdateException`, and it propagates unhandled to a generic `500` — the same failure pattern seen earlier with the unhandled `SqlException` during local setup.

**Impact:** Two-fold — (1) this is itself an unhandled-exception crash a user can trigger, which is bad regardless of auth; (2) combined with DEFECT-002, anyone unauthenticated can trigger this crash on any doctor with linked data.

**Suggested fix direction:** Once DEFECT-002's `[Authorize]` fix is in place, also add either a pre-check ("reject with `409 Conflict` if the doctor has related appointments/records") or a `try`/`catch` around the delete that returns a clean `400`/`409` instead of an unhandled `500`.

**Assigned to:** Backend Engineer

